    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Mitosis is a crucial process where a single cell divides into two identical daughter cells, ensuring the continuation of life. During this process, the cell's genetic material (chromosomes) is duplicated and equally divided, so each new cell contains the same genetic information. Mitosis occurs in four main stages: prophase, metaphase, anaphase, and telophase. Prophase is when the chromatin condenses into chromosomes, metaphase is when the chromosomes line up in the middle of the cell, anaphase is when the chromosomes are pulled to opposite ends, and telophase is when the cell divides, resulting in two new cells, each containing a full set of chromosomes. This process is essential for growth, repair, and asexual reproduction in single-celled organisms. Understanding mitosis is important as it ensures proper cellular function and stability across generations of cells.",
      "source": "target",
