      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a decent start, it's pretty accurate. But I think it's a bit too dry for a high schooler. It feels more like a textbook definition than something they'd actually want to read. \n\nCan you expand on this? Maybe go into a bit more detail for each phase (like what's happening to the spindle fibers or the nuclear envelope) so it's more thorough. \n\nAlso, just a reminder, the automated system is flagging this draft as \"low engagement\" because it's not hitting enough of those pedagogical markers (helpful, learn, understand, etc.). I really don't want you to just spam them, but can you make the tone a bit more instructional so it's clear you're trying to help them learn the material?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Mitosis is not only the backbone of life as we know it, but also a fascinating biological dance of cellular precision. Let’s dive deeper, in a way that'll help you grasp each step as if it were a story unfolding inside your textbooks. In the beginning, prophase, our story's protagonist, the cell's genetic material, condenses into tightly packed chromosomes, making it easier to manage. Simultaneously, the nuclear envelope—think of it as the cell's room divider—starts to disintegrate. Meanwhile, a new structure called the spindle, serving as our cell's movers and shakers, begins to form, setting the stage for an incredible act. When we hit metaphase, like the climax of a great movie, the spindle fibres which are essentially microscopic strings made of proteins, eagerly pull the chromosomes to line up exactly in the middle of the cell. How it feels like watching a perfectly orchestrated field goal, right?\n\nAnaphase pulls the strings of destiny tighter as these spindle fibres do their magic: pulling each sister chromatid to opposite ends of the cell, like divvying up a large pizza into two equal slices of delicious knowledge. And just as all the characters have their parts, so does the cell",
      "source": "target",
